---
import DashboardMock from "./DashboardMock.astro";
---

<section class="story" id="rhythm">
  <div class="wrap story__grid">
    <div class="story__pin">
      <DashboardMock />
      <p class="story__caption">Stays beside the work. Counts only while you are at the screen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="story__chapters">
      <article>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>Why it exists</p>
        <h2 class="display">Your eyes work hard for you. Screens make them work worse.</h2>
        <p>
          Focused computer use often cuts blink rate in half. Sparse, uneven
          blinking is a known driver of digital eye strain, dryness, grit,
          fatigue, blurred vision, and it is easy not to notice until the eyes
          already hurt. EyesRhythm is a
          <a href="/blink-rate-detector">blink rate detector</a> that sits
          beside that work.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>What rhythm means</p>
        <h2 class="display">Rate and regularity, together.</h2>
        <p>
          Rate is how often. Regularity is how steady. Enough blinks can still
          arrive in bursts and droughts; a slow but even pattern still leaves
          the surface dry. EyesRhythm folds both into the
          <strong>Ocular Rhythm Index</strong> (0 to 100). Maximize your healthy
          desk ORI, use practice daily, it helps to build muscle memory.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>The companion</p>
        <h2 class="display">It watches, scores, and, if you ask, gently nudges.</h2>
        <p>
          ORI on a compact window. Alert is always on: Visual by default, a
          quiet orange pulse, or Sound if you click A. Optional beep on each
          blink. Practice is a two-minute coach so the habit is easier to feel.
          Poise is the neck’s half of the same sit. The dial turns green where
          you looked and stays red where you did not, so a still pose gets a
          turn up, down, left, or right, and the neck and shoulders can relax
          before you face the work again. A wellness companion, not a medical
          device. Lasting discomfort belongs with an eye-care professional.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .story {
    background: var(--band);
    padding: clamp(40px, 7vw, 88px) 0;
  }

  .story__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 28px;
  }

  .story__pin {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
  }

  .story__chapters {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  article {
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
    padding: 20px 0 28px;
  }

  article p {
    color: var(--text-soft);
    max-width: 38rem;
  }

  article strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .story__caption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 22rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .story__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 1.1fr;
      align-items: start;
      gap: 56px;
    }

    .story__pin {
      position: sticky;
      top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 28px);
    }

    article {
      min-height: 0;
      align-content: start;
      padding: 28px 0 40px;
    }
  }
</style>
